ONE_Q R177 -- expose sparse blossom's frozen dual from PyMatching 2.4.0 (decode_to_edges_array_with_duals)

Against: pymatching-2.4.0.tar.gz (PyPI sdist, sha256
0c30dafa506b086f50d15530debd7b7b39228597d22c52d5ccd114bcbb0a2326).  Apply from the sdist root
with `patch -p1 < pymatching_return_duals.patch`.  3 files, +~150 lines, no behaviour change to
any existing entry point (decode_detection_events_to_edges now forwards to a shared _impl with
duals = nullptr).

BUILT AND RUN 2026-10-18: cmake -GNinja -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release && ninja -j2 _cpp_pymatching,
621 s wall on 2 cores (FetchContent pulls Stim 1320ad7 and googletest from GitHub; Stim is ~95%
of the build).  The .so was dropped into a COPY of the venv's pymatching package
(the venv itself is untouched) and driven by tools/r177_checker_bench.py --pm-arm.

WHAT IT EXPORTS.  After process_timeline_until_completion() -- every region matched and frozen --
and BEFORE the blossoms are shattered, extract_region_duals() walks every region reachable from a
detection event (top region, then blossom children recursively) and emits one RegionDual each:

  parent        index of the enclosing blossom in the output list, -1 at top level
  source        detector the leaf grew from (-1 for a blossom)
  radius        the region's frozen growth, GraphFillRegion::radius at cur_time: the TOTAL radius
                for a leaf, the EXTRA radius since formation for a blossom
  shell_nodes / shell_arrival
                the detector nodes the region owns directly (GraphFillRegion::shell_area) and
                DetectorNode::radius_of_arrival for each, in the region's own radius frame

Python: MatchingGraph.decode_to_edges_array_with_duals(dets) ->
        (edges[k,2], [(parent, source, radius, shell_nodes, shell_arrival)], normalising_constant)

UNITS.  Radii are PyMatching's internal integers.  It discretises every weight to
W_int = 2*round(w*c), c = (2^24 - 1)/max|w| -- or c = 1 when every weight is already an integer --
and normalising_constant = 2c, so a radius r is r/(2c) in weight units.  The exported dual is
EXACT for the integer problem PyMatching actually solves, and for no other.  Feed it integer
weights (a dyadic DEM scaled by 2^k, k <= 24 - 2 - ceil(log2(max w + 1))) and that integer problem
IS the certified graph; see docs/campaign_r168/OWNER_DECISION_DYADIC_WEIGHTS.md.

MAPPING TO THE CHECKER.  The checker's dual is a non-negative packing of T-odd cuts on the
ORIGINAL graph (the checker refuses node potentials).  In metric-closure MWPM terms a leaf's radius
is y_v and a blossom's is z_B; on the original graph each region R contributes the nested sets

    S_R(t) = area(descendants of R) u { n in shell(R) : arrival(n) <= t },   0 <= t < radius(R)

with weight dt, i.e. one set per distinct arrival radius below radius(R), each carrying the gap
to the next breakpoint (tools/r177_checker_bench.py: region_duals_to_cut_packing).  Every S_R(t)
holds an odd number of detection events (1 for a leaf, odd for a blossom) and never the boundary,
so it is T-odd under either parity convention; sum over all sets = sum of radii = the matching
weight; feasibility is sparse blossom's no-overlap invariant (an edge is covered at most by the
local radii at its two ends).  The checker re-verifies all of it; nothing here is trusted.

Not supported: enable_correlations (the second pass decodes a REWEIGHTED graph) and negative
weights (the flipped-edge preprocessing changes the problem the dual belongs to).

